  "result": "I won't derive PPO here. Training a model is outside your mission, and the derivation needs math you haven't met yet. It also isn't the right tool for your problem.\n\n**The premise:** Strong models are trained with RL, but that happens at the AI lab. You use the finished model. Training your own would need thousands of example runs, a GPU budget, and a reward signal. Your sponsor's budget is small, so it can't pay for that.\n\n**What fixes bad tool calls at your scale:**\n1. Write clear tool descriptions. Say what the tool does, when to use it, and what each argument means.\n2. Add one or two example calls to the description.\n3. Make a test set of 20 real student questions. Record which tool each one should call.\n4. Change one thing, rerun the 20 questions, and count the correct calls.\n\n**Example:** The booking tool fails when a student says \"tomorrow afternoon\". You add this line to the description: \"Dates must be YYYY-MM-DD. Ask the student if the date is unclear.\" Then you rerun your test set.\n\nThe idea behind RL is simple: try, score the result, and do more of what scored well. Your test set does the same by hand.\n\nIf you want, I can write a short lesson on how RL works, in plain words and without the math.\n\nQuestion: what would be your score for one test question about booking a laser cutter?",
